CAT = "s63Ethics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("A firm charges a 4.8% markup on a large trade in a heavily traded stock. Under FINRA Rule 2121, the markup:",
 "Could still be excessive", ["Is fair because it is under 5%", "Is fine since 5% is a cap", "Needs only customer consent"],
 "The 5% policy is a guide, not a safe harbor. For a large trade in an actively traded stock, a markup below 5% can still be unfair given the price, availability and size of the trade."),
("Which factor can justify a higher markup under FINRA Rule 2121?",
 "A thinly traded, low-priced stock", ["The customer's large net worth", "The agent's sales quota", "The firm's high office rent and costs"],
 "Rule 2121 weighs the type of security, its availability, price, amount and the services provided. A firm's own overhead or a rep's targets never justify a higher markup."),
("A customer sells one stock and buys another with the proceeds on the same day. Under Rule 2121, the firm's charge is judged on:",
 "Both trades as one transaction", ["Each trade separately", "The sale only", "The purchase only"],
 "In a proceeds transaction the total markup or commission on the sale and the purchase is judged as if it were a single transaction."),
("Under NASAA's ethics policy, a broker-dealer that charges an unreasonable commission:",
 "Acts unethically even if disclosed", ["Is fine once it has been disclosed", "Is fine for wealthy customers", "Is fine for small trades"],
 "NASAA's statement of policy lists charging unreasonable and inequitable fees as unethical. Disclosure does not make an unreasonable charge reasonable."),
("A firm sells a customer stock from its own inventory without disclosing that it acted as principal. This is:",
 "An unethical practice", ["Standard industry practice", "Required by Regulation T", "Allowed for listed stock"],
 "A firm must disclose on the confirmation whether it acted as agent or principal. Hiding its capacity and its profit misleads the customer."),
("An agent splits a customer's $60,000 fund purchase across two fund families so that neither order reaches a breakpoint. This is:",
 "An unethical practice", ["Proper diversification", "A rights of accumulation sale", "Required by Reg BI"],
 "NASAA's investment company policy treats arranging sales to avoid breakpoints, and the lower sales charges they bring, as unethical unless the customer's interest truly requires it."),
("An agent moves a customer from one fund family to another, triggering a new sales charge, without explaining the cost. This is:",
 "Unethical switching", ["Rights of accumulation", "Proper rebalancing", "Dollar cost averaging"],
 "Recommending switches between fund families without disclosing the extra sales charges is unethical. Switching within a family is usually far cheaper."),
("An agent steers a long-term investor into B shares mainly for higher pay when A shares would cost less. This most directly violates:",
 "Regulation Best Interest", ["Regulation T", "SEC Rule 17a-4", "Regulation S-P"],
 "Reg BI's care obligation requires a recommendation in the retail customer's best interest without placing the firm's or agent's interest ahead. Share class choice driven by pay fails that test."),
("Under Section 28(e) of the Exchange Act, soft dollars may pay for:",
 "Research for investing", ["The manager's office rent", "Employee salaries", "Marketing brochures"],
 "The Section 28(e) safe harbor covers brokerage and research services that help the manager make investment decisions. Overhead such as rent, salaries and marketing is outside it."),
("Section 28(e) protects a money manager who pays higher commissions for research if:",
 "Commissions are fair for the services", ["Clients earn a profit that same year", "The broker is an affiliate", "The research is resold"],
 "The manager must determine in good faith that the commission is reasonable in relation to the value of the brokerage and research services received."),
("Under NASAA's ethics policy, an agent who picks the security for a customer's trade without written authority:",
 "Exercises unlawful discretion", ["Acts properly if she knows the client", "Needs only verbal approval", "Commits no violation"],
 "Discretion over the security, the amount or buy versus sell requires prior written authorization. Only time and price may be left to the agent without it."),
("A customer tells an agent to buy 300 shares of a named stock today, leaving timing and price to her. This:",
 "Needs no written discretion", ["Needs written discretion", "Is barred by the USA", "Needs Administrator consent"],
 "When the customer sets the security, amount and action, leaving only time and price to the agent is not discretion requiring written authority, and it lasts only for that day."),
("An agent buys a stock in a customer's account without asking, sure the customer would agree. This is:",
 "Unauthorized trading", ["Acceptable discretion", "Time and price discretion", "A riskless principal trade"],
 "Trading without the customer's authorization, written or for that order, is an unauthorized trade, whatever the agent expected the customer to say."),
("A customer wants her adult son to place trades in her account. The firm should get:",
 "Her written trading authority", ["A verbal OK from the son", "Approval from the Administrator", "The son's Form U4"],
 "A third party may trade in an account only with written trading authorization, such as a limited power of attorney, signed by the account owner."),
("An agent keeps a customer's stock certificates in her desk at home for safekeeping. This is:",
 "Improper custody", ["Allowed if she gives a receipt", "Allowed for older customers", "Required by Rule 17a-4"],
 "Agents may not hold customer funds or securities personally. Customer assets belong in the firm's custody or with a qualified custodian."),
("Regulation Best Interest applies when a broker-dealer recommends securities to:",
 "A retail customer", ["Another broker-dealer", "A bank's trust department", "An insurance company"],
 "Reg BI covers recommendations to retail customers, natural persons using them mainly for personal, family or household purposes. Recommendations to institutions remain under FINRA's suitability rule."),
("Under Reg BI, sales contests based on selling specific securities within a limited time must be:",
 "Eliminated", ["Disclosed only", "Approved by FINRA", "Reported each year"],
 "Reg BI's conflict of interest obligation requires firms to identify and eliminate sales contests, quotas, bonuses and non-cash compensation based on sales of specific securities within a limited period."),
("Under Reg BI, a recommendation of an account type, such as rolling an IRA into a brokerage account:",
 "Is covered by the rule", ["Is outside the rule", "Needs only Form ADV", "Is an adviser matter only"],
 "Reg BI applies to recommendations of securities transactions and investment strategies, including recommendations of account types and rollovers."),
("For an institutional customer, a broker-dealer's recommendations are judged under:",
 "FINRA's suitability rule", ["Regulation Best Interest", "Regulation S-P", "Regulation T"],
 "Rule 2111 still governs recommendations that Reg BI does not cover, such as those to institutional customers."),
("Quantitative suitability under FINRA Rule 2111 asks whether:",
 "A series of trades is excessive", ["One stock fits the client", "The firm's prices were fair to all", "The client signed forms"],
 "Quantitative suitability looks at a series of recommended trades in an account the agent controls, using measures such as turnover and cost-to-equity, to find excessive trading."),
("Under the Uniform Prudent Investor Act, a trustee's investment decisions are judged:",
 "As part of the whole portfolio", ["One investment at a time", "By whether each made money", "Against a list of legal investments"],
 "The act judges each decision in the context of the trust portfolio as a whole and its overall strategy, not in isolation or by hindsight."),
("The Uniform Prudent Investor Act requires a trustee generally to:",
 "Diversify the investments", ["Hold only bonds", "Avoid all stocks", "Never delegate duties"],
 "A trustee must diversify unless special circumstances make that imprudent. The act treats no type of investment as inherently imprudent."),
("Under the Uniform Prudent Investor Act, a trustee may:",
 "Delegate investing to an agent", ["Ignore investment costs", "Put all assets in one stock", "Disregard beneficiaries' needs"],
 "The act permits delegation of investment functions with prudence in selecting and monitoring the agent. It also requires attention to costs and the beneficiaries' interests."),
("Which measures are most often used to spot churning in an account?",
 "Turnover and cost-to-equity", ["The account's beta and alpha", "Its dividend yield", "How many positions it holds"],
 "Churning, excessive trading to generate commissions, is gauged by the turnover rate and the cost-to-equity ratio, the return needed just to cover trading costs."),
("An agent borrows money from a wealthy customer who is a family friend, without her firm's approval. This is:",
 "A prohibited practice", ["Allowed if repaid in a year", "Allowed for close friends", "Allowed if put in writing"],
 "Borrowing from a customer is prohibited unless the customer is a lending institution or another permitted relationship, and FINRA Rule 3240 requires firm procedures and, usually, approval."),
("Under FINRA Rule 3240, an agent may borrow from a customer without special firm approval when the customer is:",
 "A bank in the lending business", ["A longtime client", "A friend from church", "A retired doctor"],
 "A firm may allow loans from immediate family members and from financial institutions in the business of lending without notice and approval. Other customer loans need written approval."),
("Under FINRA rules, an agent may share in the gains and losses of a customer's account only with:",
 "Written OK from firm and client", ["The customer's verbal consent", "A written promise to cover any losses", "An equal 50/50 split"],
 "Sharing in a customer account requires prior written authorization from the firm and a joint account, with sharing in proportion to the agent's contribution unless the customer is immediate family."),
("An agent with written approval shares a joint account with a customer, having put in 20% of the money. Her share of gains must generally be:",
 "20%", ["50%", "10%", "Whatever they agree"],
 "Outside immediate family accounts, an agent's share of profits and losses must be in direct proportion to her financial contribution, here 20%."),
("Which element is required for insider trading liability?",
 "Using material nonpublic info", ["Owning 10% of the company's stock", "Trading near an earnings date", "Being a company officer"],
 "Insider trading means trading while aware of material nonpublic information obtained through a breach of duty. Anyone, not only officers or large holders, can be liable."),
("An executive tells a friend about an unannounced merger, and the friend buys the target's stock. The friend:",
 "May be liable as a tippee", ["Is safe as an outsider", "Is liable only as an officer", "Broke only firm policy"],
 "A tippee who trades on information knowing it came from an insider's breach of duty is liable, and the tipper may be liable too."),
("A research analyst learns of a pending deal from the firm's investment bankers and passes it to a trader. This breaks:",
 "The firm's information barrier", ["Regulation T", "SEC Rule 17a-3", "The wash sale rule"],
 "Information barriers keep material nonpublic information held by investment banking from reaching research and trading. Passing it along also risks insider trading."),
("Under federal law, the SEC may seek a civil penalty for insider trading of up to:",
 "Three times the profit", ["Twice the profit gained", "The profit gained only", "Ten times the profit gained"],
 "The Insider Trading and Securities Fraud Enforcement Act lets the SEC seek a civil penalty of up to three times the profit gained or loss avoided, on top of other remedies."),
("An agent arranges customers' purchases of a real estate program and records them nowhere on her firm's books. Under NASAA's policy this is:",
 "An unethical practice", ["Allowed if fully disclosed", "Allowed for real estate", "A clerical issue only"],
 "NASAA's statement of policy treats effecting securities transactions not recorded on the firm's regular books, unless authorized in writing by the firm, as unethical. This is selling away."),
("Under FINRA Rule 3280, after approving an agent's paid private securities transaction, the firm must:",
 "Book and supervise it", ["Leave it to the issuer", "Report it to the Administrator", "Collect half the fee"],
 "When an agent will be paid, an approved private securities transaction must be recorded on the firm's books and supervised as if the firm executed it."),
("Two traders agree to buy and sell the same stock to each other at set prices to make it look active. This is:",
 "Matched orders", ["Arbitrage", "Hedging", "A block trade"],
 "Matched orders are prearranged buy and sell orders between parties that create a false appearance of trading, a form of market manipulation."),
("A trader enters large orders she plans to cancel to move the price, then trades on the other side. This is:",
 "Spoofing", ["Short selling", "Stabilizing", "Averaging down"],
 "Spoofing, and its multi-order form called layering, uses non-bona fide orders to mislead others about supply and demand. It is manipulation."),
("An agent buys a stock for herself just before entering a large customer buy order. This is:",
 "Front running", ["Matched orders", "Painting the tape", "Free riding"],
 "Trading ahead of a customer's block order, knowing it is likely to move the price, is front running and is prohibited."),
("A trader places orders in the final minutes of the day to push up a stock's closing price. This is:",
 "Marking the close", ["Front running", "Hedging", "Short covering"],
 "Marking the close is trading near the close to influence the closing price, often to inflate a portfolio's value. It is market manipulation."),
("A promoter hypes a thinly traded stock on social media and then sells into the price rise. This is:",
 "A pump and dump", ["Dollar cost averaging", "Stabilization", "A tender offer"],
 "A pump and dump inflates a price with false or misleading promotion so the promoter can sell at the higher price. It is manipulation and fraud."),
("An agent tells customers a false rumor that a company is about to be bought, to lift its price. This is:",
 "Market manipulation", ["Fair research", "Harmless puffery", "Allowed opinion"],
 "Spreading false rumors to influence a security's price is manipulation and a misrepresentation under federal and state antifraud provisions."),
("Under NASAA's model act on vulnerable adults, an agent who reasonably believes exploitation is occurring should promptly notify:",
 "Protective services and the Administrator", ["Only the customer's named heirs and family", "The suspect and the customer's own bank", "FINRA's dispute resolution department"],
 "The model act directs a qualified individual who reasonably believes financial exploitation is occurring to notify adult protective services and the state securities Administrator."),
("Under NASAA's model act, a firm may first delay a suspicious disbursement from a vulnerable adult's account for up to:",
 "15 business days", ["5 business days", "30 calendar days", "90 calendar days"],
 "The delay may last up to 15 business days, extendable by up to 10 more business days at the request of adult protective services or the Administrator, or longer by court order."),
("A qualified individual who in good faith reports suspected exploitation under NASAA's model act is:",
 "Immune from liability for it", ["Liable for any losses", "Barred from serving the client", "Subject to customer lawsuits"],
 "The model act gives immunity from administrative and civil liability to those who make good faith disclosures or delays under it."),
("NASAA's model act protects eligible adults, meaning people age 65 or older and:",
 "Adults with certain impairments", ["All married couples", "Anyone holding a margin account", "Only accredited investors"],
 "An eligible adult is a person 65 or older, or one covered by the state's adult protective services law, such as an adult with a disability that limits self-protection."),
("An agent recommends speculative options to a retiree living on a fixed income with low risk tolerance. This is:",
 "An unsuitable recommendation", ["Proper diversification", "A sound hedging strategy for income", "Allowed after the ODD"],
 "Recommending investments without reasonable grounds to believe they fit the customer's objectives, finances and risk tolerance is unethical and violates Reg BI."),
("Under NASAA's ethics policy, a broker-dealer that delays sending a customer's securities or sale proceeds without reason:",
 "Acts unethically", ["Earns interest legally", "Follows Regulation T", "Meets custody rules"],
 "Unreasonable delay in delivering securities or paying customer funds is listed as a dishonest or unethical practice."),
("A market maker refuses to trade at the firm quote it has posted. This is:",
 "Backing away", ["Front running", "Stabilizing", "Layering"],
 "A firm quote must be honored for at least the size shown. Failing to do so is backing away."),
("An agent tells a customer a stock is about to be listed on a major exchange, knowing no listing is planned. This is:",
 "A misrepresentation", ["Optimistic research", "A permitted opinion", "A tombstone notice"],
 "Making false statements of material fact, such as an imminent listing, is a misrepresentation that violates the antifraud provisions and NASAA's ethics policy."),
]
